window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["azure-ai"]=[{"id":"azure-ai-f0001","card":"q-azure-ai-001-1-big-picture","sec":"azure-ai-s002","q":"What are Azure AI services, and when would you use them instead of training your own model?","a":"<p>They are pre-built AI APIs (vision, speech, language and others) that you call over REST or an SDK (.NET, Python, JavaScript, Java). You need no machine-learning background. Use them when the task is a common one (OCR, transcription, sentiment) and a general model is accurate enough. Train or fine-tune your own model only when you need domain accuracy the pre-built API cannot give, or full control over the model. Naming note: the family was called Cognitive Services before it was renamed Azure AI Services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0002","card":"q-azure-ai-001-1-big-picture","sec":"azure-ai-s002","q":"How do the three levels of working with AI differ: AI services, AI solutions built on existing models, and AI models trained from scratch?","a":"<p>AI services are ready-made APIs you just call. AI solutions start from a model someone else trained and adapt it (prompting, RAG, fine-tuning, distillation), which is where Azure AI Foundry sits. Training models from scratch needs huge datasets and GPU clusters, and only a few large labs do it. Cost, effort and control rise as you go from the first to the last.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0003","card":"q-azure-ai-001-1-big-picture","sec":"azure-ai-s002","q":"Which steps do you follow to call any Azure AI service from your own application for the first time?","a":"<p>Create the resource in the Azure portal (a free tier is fine for development), open Keys and Endpoint, and copy a key and the endpoint (some services also need the region). Keep them in environment variables or a secret store, not in source code, then create the SDK client with them and call it. In production prefer Microsoft Entra ID or managed identity over keys where the service supports it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0004","card":"q-azure-ai-001-1-big-picture","sec":"azure-ai-s002","q":"Why does an Azure AI resource give you two keys?","a":"<p>Two keys let you rotate without downtime. You move clients to key 2, regenerate key 1, then move clients back. If both clients and the rotation used a single key, regenerating it would break running apps until they were redeployed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0005","card":"q-azure-ai-002-2-vision","sec":"azure-ai-s003","q":"Which Azure AI Vision capabilities exist for still images, and which would you pick for reading a receipt versus describing a photo?","a":"<p>Vision covers OCR, Image Analysis (captions, tags, objects) and Face. For describing a photo use Image Analysis. For a receipt use OCR (or Document Intelligence if you need fields like total and merchant). Video-oriented features such as Spatial Analysis have changed status over time, so check the current docs before relying on them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0006","card":"q-azure-ai-003-ocr","sec":"azure-ai-s004","q":"What is OCR in Azure AI Vision, and what does it return?","a":"<p>OCR extracts printed and handwritten text from images and documents in many languages. The result lists pages, then lines, then words. Each line and word has a bounding polygon (its position) and each word has a confidence score between 0 and 1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0007","card":"q-azure-ai-003-ocr","sec":"azure-ai-s004","q":"Why does the OCR result include a confidence score per word, and how would you use it?","a":"<p>Text can be blurred, overlapping or partly hidden, so the engine is not equally sure about every word. You set a threshold, accept words that score higher, and send low-confidence words or documents to human review or ask for a better image. Without it you would trust wrong text silently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0008","card":"q-azure-ai-003-ocr","sec":"azure-ai-s004","q":"Why is the bounding polygon in an OCR result useful?","a":"<p>It gives the position of each line or word. You can highlight text on the original image, redact regions, map text to a form layout, or tell which text belongs to which area of a page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0009","card":"q-azure-ai-003-ocr","sec":"azure-ai-s004","q":"How is the OCR response structured for a multi-page PDF or TIFF?","a":"<p>The result holds an array of pages. Each page has lines, and each line has words with text, bounding box and confidence. A JPEG gives one page and a PDF or TIFF gives one entry per page, so code must loop over pages, not assume one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0010","card":"q-azure-ai-003-ocr","sec":"azure-ai-s004","q":"When can OCR run on-premises instead of in the cloud, and why would you want that?","a":"<p>Azure AI Vision Read OCR is offered as a Docker container that you run on your own hardware or at the edge. You do this when data cannot leave your network for compliance reasons, when connectivity is poor, or when latency must be very low. You still need to connect the container to an Azure resource for billing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0011","card":"q-azure-ai-003-ocr","sec":"azure-ai-s004","q":"When should you use Document Intelligence instead of plain OCR?","a":"<p>Use OCR when you only need the raw text. Use Document Intelligence when you need structure: key-value pairs, tables, and typed fields from invoices, receipts or IDs. Plain OCR returns text and positions but does not know that a number is the invoice total.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0012","card":"q-azure-ai-004-image-analysis","sec":"azure-ai-s005","q":"What kinds of information can Azure AI Vision Image Analysis return about a picture?","a":"<p>Version matters. Image Analysis 4.0 returns caption, dense captions, tags, object detection, people detection, read (OCR) and smart-cropped thumbnails. The older Image Analysis 3.2 API also offered brands, landmarks, celebrities, image type, color scheme and adult/racy/gory flags. You choose which features to request in the call. Check the current docs for what your API version supports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0013","card":"q-azure-ai-004-image-analysis","sec":"azure-ai-s005","q":"Why does an Image Analysis request ask you to name the features you want?","a":"<p>Each feature is a separate analysis with its own cost and latency. Naming only what you need keeps the call faster and cheaper, and the response stays small. Asking for everything on every image wastes money.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0014","card":"q-azure-ai-004-image-analysis","sec":"azure-ai-s005","q":"What is smart cropping in Image Analysis and where would you use it?","a":"<p>It finds the most important region of an image (a face or main subject) and returns crop coordinates for a chosen aspect ratio. Use it to build thumbnails that keep the subject in frame instead of cutting from the center.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0015","card":"q-azure-ai-004-image-analysis","sec":"azure-ai-s005","q":"How is a caption different from tags in Image Analysis?","a":"<p>A caption is one sentence describing the whole scene (\"a dog sticking its head out of a car window\"). Tags are separate keywords with confidence scores (\"dog\", \"car\", \"outdoor\"). Use captions for accessibility text and tags for search and filtering.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0016","card":"q-azure-ai-005-custom-vision-build-your-own-models","sec":"azure-ai-s006","q":"What are the two project types in Azure AI Custom Vision, and how do they differ?","a":"<p>Image classification gives one or more labels for the whole image. Object detection gives labels plus coordinates (bounding boxes) of where each object appears. Use classification for \"is this a damaged part\" and detection for \"where are the damaged parts and how many\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0017","card":"q-azure-ai-005-custom-vision-build-your-own-models","sec":"azure-ai-s006","q":"When do you choose a custom vision model over the pre-built Image Analysis API?","a":"<p>When your categories are specific to your business (your product lines, a specific defect type), or the generic model is not accurate enough on your images. The trade-off is that you must collect and label training images and retrain when things change. Version note: Microsoft has announced the retirement of Azure AI Custom Vision (planned for 2028), with Azure AI Vision custom models and Foundry as the direction, so check the current docs before starting a new project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0018","card":"q-azure-ai-005-custom-vision-build-your-own-models","sec":"azure-ai-s006","q":"In the Azure portal, why can picking \"Custom Vision\" instead of \"Computer Vision\" be the wrong choice for reading text from an image?","a":"<p>Custom Vision is for training your own classification or detection models. Reading text uses the pre-built Azure AI Vision (formerly Computer Vision) resource. Picking the wrong resource means the key and endpoint do not serve the API you are calling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0019","card":"q-azure-ai-005-custom-vision-build-your-own-models","sec":"azure-ai-s006","q":"How would you improve a custom image classifier that confuses two classes?","a":"<p>Add more varied training images of both classes (angles, lighting, backgrounds), make sure classes are balanced and labels are correct, test on images the model has not seen, and compare precision and recall per class. Do not judge only on training images because that hides overfitting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0020","card":"q-azure-ai-006-face-service","sec":"azure-ai-s007","q":"Why is access to the Azure AI Face service restricted?","a":"<p>Face recognition has privacy, bias and surveillance risks, so Microsoft applies Limited Access under its responsible AI policy. Customers apply through an intake form and must show an approved use case. Capabilities such as identification and verification need approval, not just a resource.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0021","card":"q-azure-ai-006-face-service","sec":"azure-ai-s007","q":"What is liveness detection in Face, and what attack does it stop?","a":"<p>It checks that a live person is in front of the camera and not a photo, a replayed video or a 3D mask. This is anti-spoofing. Without it, someone could pass identity verification with a printed picture of the victim.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0022","card":"q-azure-ai-006-face-service","sec":"azure-ai-s007","q":"How would you build a touchless access control flow with the Face service?","a":"<p>Combine liveness detection (a real person is present) with identity verification (the face matches the trusted image on file, such as a photo from an ID). Both are needed: verification alone can be fooled by a photo, and liveness alone does not tell you who the person is.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0023","card":"q-azure-ai-006-face-service","sec":"azure-ai-s007","q":"What does a Face detect call return for an image with several people?","a":"<p>An array with one entry per detected face. Each entry has a face rectangle (left, top, width, height), a face ID, landmarks (for example pupils, nose tip, eyebrows, lips) and optional attributes such as head pose, blur, exposure, glasses, occlusion and mask. Your code must loop over the array.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0024","card":"q-azure-ai-006-face-service","sec":"azure-ai-s007","q":"What is face redaction used for?","a":"<p>It blurs or removes faces in images or video to protect privacy, for example before sharing footage. It is the opposite of recognition: you detect faces to hide them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0025","card":"q-azure-ai-007-demo-handwriting-recognition-exercise-01","sec":"azure-ai-s008","q":"In the Azure AI Vision Read API for Node.js (<code>@azure/cognitiveservices-computervision</code>), why does the code poll <code>getReadResult</code> in a loop after calling <code>readInStream</code>?","a":"<p>Read is asynchronous. The first call only starts the job and returns an operation location, not text. The code extracts the operation ID, waits (for example one second), and calls <code>getReadResult</code> until the status is <code>succeeded</code>. Reading results too early returns a running or not-started status with no text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0026","card":"q-azure-ai-007-demo-handwriting-recognition-exercise-01","sec":"azure-ai-s008","q":"In the Azure AI Vision Read API for Node.js, when do you use <code>readInStream</code> and when <code>read</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>readInStream</code> when you send the image bytes (a local file stream). Use <code>read</code> when you pass a URL that the service can reach publicly. Both start the same asynchronous job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0027","card":"q-azure-ai-007-demo-handwriting-recognition-exercise-01","sec":"azure-ai-s008","q":"In an Azure AI Vision client for Node.js, what does <code>ApiKeyCredentials</code> with <code>Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key</code> do?","a":"<p>It tells the client to send your resource key in the <code>Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key</code> HTTP header on every request. That header is how Azure AI services authenticate key-based calls. A wrong or missing key gives a 401 error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>new ApiKeyCredentials({ inHeader: { &#x27;Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key&#x27;: key } })</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"azure-ai-f0028","card":"q-azure-ai-007-demo-handwriting-recognition-exercise-01","sec":"azure-ai-s008","q":"Why should the Azure AI Vision key and endpoint come from a <code>.env</code> file loaded by <code>dotenv</code> and not be written in the script?","a":"<p>Keys in source code leak through version control and logs. A <code>.env</code> file kept out of git (listed in <code>.gitignore</code>) keeps them out of the repository, and the same code runs in different environments with different values. In production use a secret store such as Key Vault or managed identity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0029","card":"q-azure-ai-008-3-speech","sec":"azure-ai-s009","q":"Which capabilities does Azure AI Speech offer, and how do you pick the right one for a call-center product?","a":"<p>Speech to text, text to speech, speech translation, speaker recognition and intent recognition. A call-center product usually needs speech to text (transcripts), maybe speaker recognition or diarization, and text to speech for automated replies. Add translation for multilingual callers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0030","card":"q-azure-ai-008-3-speech","sec":"azure-ai-s009","q":"In the Speech SDK for JavaScript, why does <code>SpeechConfig.fromSubscription</code> take both a key and a region?","a":"<p>Speech endpoints are regional. The region tells the SDK which regional endpoint to call, and the key authenticates to the resource in that region. A key with the wrong region gives an authentication failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0031","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"What are the three ways to run Azure AI Speech to text, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Real-time transcribes as audio arrives from a microphone or stream, for live captions. Fast transcription is synchronous and returns faster than the audio length, good for a recorded file you need quickly. Batch transcription is asynchronous: you submit many files and poll for results, good for large volumes where latency does not matter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0032","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"When is Custom Speech worth it over the out-of-the-box speech model, and what does it cost you?","a":"<p>Use it when the audio has domain terms, product names, accents or noisy conditions the base model gets wrong. It improves accuracy, but you need audio and transcript training data, training time, cost, and you must maintain and retest the model as base models change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0033","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"What is the workflow for building a Custom Speech model?","a":"<p>Collect audio and matching transcripts, prepare them (format, noise handling, segmentation), create the model (acoustic, language or pronunciation customization), train it, test on data it has not seen and compare with the baseline model, then deploy to an endpoint. Testing against the baseline shows whether customization actually helped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0034","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"How do you decide between acoustic, language and pronunciation customization in Custom Speech?","a":"<p>Acoustic customization helps when the audio conditions are different (noise, accents, microphones). Language customization helps when the words are different (jargon, product names), and it needs only text. Pronunciation customization helps for specific words that are said in unusual ways, such as acronyms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0035","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"What happens to Custom Speech models over time as Microsoft releases new base models?","a":"<p>Base models are released on a regular schedule and old ones expire. A custom model built on an expired base model stops being available for transcription, so you must retrain on a newer base model. Treat model maintenance as ongoing work. Version note: check the current docs for exact expiry periods, because they have changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0036","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"In the Speech SDK for JavaScript, what is the difference between <code>recognizeOnceAsync</code> and continuous recognition?","a":"<p><code>recognizeOnceAsync</code> listens until the first utterance ends (a short pause, or at most about 30 seconds) and returns one result. Continuous recognition (<code>startContinuousRecognitionAsync</code>) keeps going and raises events for each recognized phrase, so use it for long audio or live meetings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0037","card":"q-azure-ai-009-speech-to-text","sec":"azure-ai-s010","q":"In the Speech SDK, how should you handle the three <code>ResultReason</code> outcomes <code>RecognizedSpeech</code>, <code>NoMatch</code> and <code>Canceled</code> after a recognition call?","a":"<p><code>RecognizedSpeech</code> means text is in <code>result.text</code>. <code>NoMatch</code> means audio was received but no speech could be recognized, which is not an error. <code>Canceled</code> means something failed (bad key, wrong region, network); read <code>CancellationDetails.fromResult(result)</code> for the reason and error details. Handle all three, and close the recognizer afterward.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0038","card":"q-azure-ai-010-text-to-speech","sec":"azure-ai-s011","q":"What are the three kinds of voices in Azure AI Speech text to speech, and when would you use each?","a":"<p>Prebuilt neural voices are ready for everyone and fit most apps. Custom neural voice is trained on your own recordings (about one to two hours of data) to give a brand voice. Personal voice clones a specific person's voice from about one minute of their speech plus a consent statement. Custom and personal voices need Microsoft approval because of misuse risk such as impersonation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0039","card":"q-azure-ai-010-text-to-speech","sec":"azure-ai-s011","q":"What are visemes in Speech text to speech, and what are they for?","a":"<p>Visemes describe mouth and lip positions that match the synthesized audio. You use them to animate an avatar or talking face so the lips match the speech.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0040","card":"q-azure-ai-010-text-to-speech","sec":"azure-ai-s011","q":"What can SSML do for Azure text to speech that plain text cannot?","a":"<p>SSML (Speech Synthesis Markup Language) lets you pick the voice, speaking style (cheerful, newscast and so on, where the voice supports it), speed, pitch, pauses and pronunciation. Plain text gets default delivery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0041","card":"q-azure-ai-010-text-to-speech","sec":"azure-ai-s011","q":"In the Speech SDK for JavaScript, which pieces do you need to turn text into an audio file?","a":"<p>A <code>SpeechConfig</code> (key, region, voice name such as <code>en-US-JennyNeural</code>, output format), an <code>AudioConfig</code> that points at the output (<code>fromAudioFileOutput</code>), a <code>SpeechSynthesizer</code> built from both, and a call to <code>speakTextAsync</code>. Close the synthesizer after the result arrives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0042","card":"q-azure-ai-011-speaker-recognition","sec":"azure-ai-s012","q":"What is the difference between speaker identification and speaker verification in Azure AI Speech?","a":"<p>Identification answers \"who is speaking\" by matching a voice against a group of enrolled profiles. Verification answers \"is this person who they claim to be\" by comparing the voice to a single enrolled profile, for example voice login. Verification is a one-to-one check and identification is one-to-many.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0043","card":"q-azure-ai-011-speaker-recognition","sec":"azure-ai-s012","q":"What is the flow for speaker recognition, and why must the test sample be new audio?","a":"<p>You create a profile for each speaker and enroll it with enough speech (for example 30 or more seconds), then submit a new sample and the service returns the best matching profile. A new sample is needed because testing on the enrollment audio would match trivially and tell you nothing about real accuracy. Version note: Speaker Recognition is a Limited Access feature, so check current availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0044","card":"q-azure-ai-012-speech-translation","sec":"azure-ai-s013","q":"What are the output options for Azure AI Speech translation?","a":"<p>Speech to translated text (English audio to French text), speech to translated speech (the listener hears their own language), and multilingual translation that detects each spoken language and translates into several target languages. Pick text output for captions and speech output for live interpretation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0045","card":"q-azure-ai-012-speech-translation","sec":"azure-ai-s013","q":"What problem does multilingual speech translation solve compared with fixing one source language?","a":"<p>With a fixed source language the service fails when someone switches language. Multilingual translation detects the language of each utterance, so a meeting with people speaking different languages can be translated on the fly without configuring each speaker.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0046","card":"q-azure-ai-013-intent-recognition","sec":"azure-ai-s014","q":"What are the two ways to do intent recognition with Azure AI Speech, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Pattern matching is strict, fast, works offline, and returns an error for no match, so it suits fixed commands (\"go to floor nine\") or IVR menus. CLU (Conversational Language Understanding) handles free natural phrasing and extracts intent and entities, so it suits open conversation. Choose patterns for a small fixed command set and CLU for varied phrasing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0047","card":"q-azure-ai-013-intent-recognition","sec":"azure-ai-s014","q":"What is the difference between an intent and an entity in conversational language understanding?","a":"<p>The intent is what the user wants to do (<code>get_price</code>, <code>buy</code>, <code>sell</code>). Entities are the details needed to do it (the stock symbol, a date, a quantity). \"What is the price of Oracle?\" gives intent <code>get_price</code> and entity <code>ORCL</code>. Many phrasings map to the same intent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0048","card":"q-azure-ai-013-intent-recognition","sec":"azure-ai-s014","q":"How do you build a CLU model for voice commands?","a":"<p>Define intents and entities, add example utterances for each intent and label the entities, train the model, test it and deploy it. In Speech, you then give the deployed model to the intent recognizer, submit an utterance and read the intent and entities from the result. More varied examples improve accuracy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0049","card":"q-azure-ai-013-intent-recognition","sec":"azure-ai-s014","q":"What replaced LUIS for intent recognition in Azure?","a":"<p>Conversational Language Understanding (CLU) in Azure AI Language replaced LUIS. LUIS was deprecated and has a retirement schedule, so new projects should use CLU and existing LUIS apps should be migrated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0050","card":"q-azure-ai-014-demo-tts-and-stt-exercise-02-07b","sec":"azure-ai-s015","q":"In the Speech SDK for JavaScript, why does the recognizer code close the <code>SpeechRecognizer</code> at the end of the callback?","a":"<p>Closing releases the audio input and network resources held by the recognizer. Without it, the process may keep resources open or hang, and many recognizers would leak connections. Close it after you have read the result.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>speechRecognizer.recognizeOnceAsync(result =&gt; {\n    // ... handle result.reason ...\n    speechRecognizer.close();\n});</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"azure-ai-f0051","card":"q-azure-ai-014-demo-tts-and-stt-exercise-02-07b","sec":"azure-ai-s015","q":"In the Speech SDK for JavaScript, what role does <code>AudioConfig</code> play, and how do the TTS and STT variants differ?","a":"<p><code>AudioConfig</code> says where audio comes from or goes to. For speech to text you use an input such as <code>AudioConfig.fromWavFileInput(...)</code>. For text to speech you use an output such as <code>AudioConfig.fromAudioFileOutput(\"file\")</code>. Without it, the SDK uses the default microphone or speaker.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0052","card":"q-azure-ai-015-4-language","sec":"azure-ai-s016","q":"What is Azure AI Language and which families of capability does it include?","a":"<p>It is the service for natural language text. Families: extract information (key phrases, entities, PII), summarize, classify (sentiment, language detection, custom classification), question answering, conversational language understanding (CLU) and translation. It turns free text into structured results your code can use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0053","card":"q-azure-ai-015-4-language","sec":"azure-ai-s016","q":"When would you use a pre-built Azure AI Language feature and when a custom one?","a":"<p>Use pre-built features (sentiment, key phrases, NER) when general behavior fits. Use custom features (custom NER, custom text classification, CLU) when you need your own labels or domain terms. Custom needs labeled data and training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0054","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"What is the difference between key phrase extraction and named entity recognition (NER) in Azure AI Language?","a":"<p>Key phrase extraction returns the main talking points (for example \"eye prescription\") with no type. NER finds specific items and assigns a category such as person, location, organization, date. Use key phrases to summarize topics and NER when you need typed values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0055","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"What problem does entity linking solve?","a":"<p>Names are ambiguous: \"Mercury\" could be a planet, an element or a person. Entity linking connects the mention to a knowledge-base entry (such as a Wikipedia page) using context, so the meaning is disambiguated and you get a stable identifier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0056","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"How would you remove personal data from support transcripts before storing them?","a":"<p>Use PII detection in Azure AI Language. It finds and categorizes sensitive items (names, card numbers, SSNs, phone numbers) and returns a redacted version of the text. Store the redacted text, and confirm detection quality on your own data because no detector catches everything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0057","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"When would you use custom NER?","a":"<p>When the entities you need are not in the pre-built categories, for example part numbers, clause types in contracts, or drug names in your own taxonomy. You label example documents, train, evaluate, and improve in a loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0058","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"What does Text Analytics for Health add over general NER?","a":"<p>It targets medical text. It extracts clinical entities (diagnosis, medication, symptoms, age), relations between them, links entities to the UMLS Metathesaurus, and detects assertions such as negated, uncertain or conditional (\"no sign of pneumonia\"). General NER would not catch that negation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0059","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"Why does assertion detection matter in clinical text?","a":"<p>The same word can mean opposite things: \"patient denies chest pain\" and \"patient has chest pain\" both contain the symptom. Assertion detection marks certainty, negation, conditionality and who or when it applies, so downstream logic does not record a symptom the patient does not have.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0060","card":"q-azure-ai-016-extract-information","sec":"azure-ai-s017","q":"In the older Node.js package <code>@azure/ai-text-analytics</code> (the newer one is <code>@azure/ai-language-text</code> with <code>TextAnalysisClient</code>), what do you pass to <code>client.extractKeyPhrases</code> and what does each result contain?","a":"<p>Pass an array of strings (documents). It returns a promise of one result per document, each with an <code>id</code> and either <code>keyPhrases</code> or an <code>error</code>. Check <code>error</code> per document, because one bad document does not fail the whole batch.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const client = new TextAnalyticsClient(endpoint, new AzureKeyCredential(key));\nconst results = client.extractKeyPhrases(documents);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"azure-ai-f0061","card":"q-azure-ai-017-summarize","sec":"azure-ai-s018","q":"What is the difference between extractive and abstractive summarization in Azure AI Language?","a":"<p>Extractive summarization selects the most important existing sentences and returns them unchanged, so it is faithful to the text. Abstractive summarization writes new sentences that capture the meaning, so it reads more naturally but can introduce errors that are not in the source. Choose extractive when exact wording matters and abstractive when readability matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0062","card":"q-azure-ai-017-summarize","sec":"azure-ai-s018","q":"Which conversation summary types does Azure AI Language offer, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Recap (a short paragraph of the conversation), issue and resolution (for call centers: the problem and how it was solved), chapter title (splits by topic with titles), and narrative (detailed notes of a call, meeting or chat). Pick by the audience: agents reviewing cases want issue and resolution, meeting readers want narrative.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0063","card":"q-azure-ai-018-classify","sec":"azure-ai-s019","q":"What does sentiment analysis in Azure AI Language return, and what is opinion mining on top of it?","a":"<p>Sentiment analysis labels text positive, neutral, negative (also mixed at document level) with confidence scores per sentence. Opinion mining links sentiment to specific aspects, for example \"food: positive, service: negative\" in one review. Use it to see what exactly customers like or dislike.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0064","card":"q-azure-ai-018-classify","sec":"azure-ai-s019","q":"What does language detection return, and why can it return a script?","a":"<p>It returns the detected language with a confidence score. Some languages are written in more than one script, so the result also reports the script, for example when a language can appear in Latin or another writing system.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0065","card":"q-azure-ai-018-classify","sec":"azure-ai-s019","q":"When do you need custom text classification instead of sentiment analysis?","a":"<p>When the categories are your own, such as support-ticket type, book genre or email triage. Sentiment only says positive or negative. Custom classification needs labeled examples, training and evaluation, and can be single-label or multi-label.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0066","card":"q-azure-ai-019-question-answering","sec":"azure-ai-s020","q":"What does Azure AI Language question answering do, and how is it different from asking a large language model directly?","a":"<p>It builds a question-and-answer knowledge base from your own documents, FAQs and URLs, and returns answers taken from that content. It answers only from your data, so responses are controlled and predictable. A plain LLM answers from its training data and may invent facts. Many solutions now combine retrieval over your data with an LLM instead (RAG).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0067","card":"q-azure-ai-019-question-answering","sec":"azure-ai-s020","q":"What is multi-turn conversation in question answering?","a":"<p>The bot can ask follow-up prompts to narrow down the user's need, such as asking which processor family before giving details. You define follow-up prompts on question pairs so one vague question leads to the right answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0068","card":"q-azure-ai-019-question-answering","sec":"azure-ai-s020","q":"How do you improve the quality of a question answering knowledge base over time?","a":"<p>Add alternate phrasings for questions, add metadata for filtering, add more source content, and test with real user questions in a loop. Review unanswered or low-confidence queries and turn them into new question pairs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0069","card":"q-azure-ai-019-question-answering","sec":"azure-ai-s020","q":"What service did Azure AI Language question answering replace?","a":"<p>QnA Maker. QnA Maker was retired, and question answering in Azure AI Language is the successor with the same idea and a migration path. Version note: confirm the current state in Microsoft docs before planning a migration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0070","card":"q-azure-ai-020-clu","sec":"azure-ai-s021","q":"What does Conversational Language Understanding (CLU) in Azure AI Language predict?","a":"<p>For a text utterance it predicts the top intent and extracts entities. \"Book a flight to Paris tomorrow\" gives intent <code>book_flight</code> and entities for destination and date. Your application then maps the intent to an action.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0071","card":"q-azure-ai-020-clu","sec":"azure-ai-s021","q":"How would you handle an utterance that matches none of your CLU intents?","a":"<p>Add a <code>None</code> intent trained with out-of-scope examples, and use the confidence score with a threshold. If the score is under the threshold, ask the user to rephrase or hand off to a person, rather than running the wrong action.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0072","card":"q-azure-ai-020-clu","sec":"azure-ai-s021","q":"How many example utterances and what kind of variety should a CLU intent have?","a":"<p>A handful of varied examples per intent is the minimum, and more varied ones improve results. Vary wording, length and entity values. Near-identical examples add little. Also keep intents clearly distinct, since overlapping intents confuse the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0073","card":"q-azure-ai-021-demo-key-phrase-extraction-exercise-03-0","sec":"azure-ai-s022","q":"In Azure AI Language with the older <code>@azure/ai-text-analytics</code> package, why must your code check <code>result.error</code> for each document in the response?","a":"<p>A batch call can succeed overall while some documents fail (for example empty text or too long). Each result carries either <code>keyPhrases</code> or an <code>error</code>, so reading <code>keyPhrases</code> without checking would fail for the bad documents. Handle errors per document.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0074","card":"q-azure-ai-021-demo-key-phrase-extraction-exercise-03-0","sec":"azure-ai-s022","q":"In Azure AI Language, what are the main limits to plan for when sending many documents to key phrase extraction?","a":"<p>Requests accept a limited number of documents per call and a limited number of characters per document, and the service has rate limits per tier. Split large sets into batches and respect the current limits listed in the docs, which change over time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0075","card":"q-azure-ai-022-5-search","sec":"azure-ai-s023","q":"When do you choose Azure AI Search over Microsoft Search, Bing Search, or searching inside your database?","a":"<p>Microsoft Search is for M365 and SharePoint content. Bing Search covers the open internet. Database search (SQL full-text, Cosmos DB) is fine when data is already in one store and needs are simple. Choose Azure AI Search for your own content from many stores, relevance tuning, heavy query load that you want off the database, or AI enrichment and vector search. Naming note: it was called Azure Cognitive Search before.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0076","card":"q-azure-ai-022-5-search","sec":"azure-ai-s023","q":"How does Azure AI Search work end to end, from data to query?","a":"<p>Provision a search service (choose tier, replicas and partitions), create an index, load content with an indexer or push API (a full crawl first, then incremental refreshes of changes), optionally run AI enrichment, then applications query the index through REST, SDKs or the portal. Queries run against the index, not the original data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0077","card":"q-azure-ai-022-5-search","sec":"azure-ai-s023","q":"Why is querying an Azure AI Search index fast compared with querying the original data stores?","a":"<p>The data is copied into a purpose-built index (inverted index for text, vector index for embeddings) that is designed for search and ranking. The original databases or files are not scanned at query time, and search load stays off your operational database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0078","card":"q-azure-ai-022-5-search","sec":"azure-ai-s023","q":"In Azure AI Search, what is the difference between an index, an indexer, a data source and a skillset?","a":"<p>The index holds the searchable documents and the field schema. A data source defines where the content lives. An indexer pulls from the data source into the index on a schedule. A skillset is an optional set of AI skills the indexer applies while building the index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0079","card":"q-azure-ai-023-search-types","sec":"azure-ai-s024","q":"What is the difference between full-text, vector and hybrid search in Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>Full-text matches words after analysis (lowercasing, stop-word removal, stemming) and ranks with BM25. Vector search compares embeddings so it finds similar meaning even without shared words. Hybrid runs both in parallel and merges the rankings (reciprocal rank fusion).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0080","card":"q-azure-ai-023-search-types","sec":"azure-ai-s024","q":"Why is hybrid search usually better than vector-only search for enterprise content?","a":"<p>Vector search can miss exact terms such as product codes, names or error numbers, while full-text catches them. Full-text misses paraphrases that vectors find. Combining them covers both, and adding semantic ranking on top usually improves the order further.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0081","card":"q-azure-ai-023-search-types","sec":"azure-ai-s024","q":"What happens to the query \"the running dogs\" in a full-text index, and why does it still match \"dog runs\"?","a":"<p>The analyzer lowercases the text, removes stop words such as \"the\", and reduces words to root forms (stemming), so \"running\" becomes \"run\" and \"dogs\" becomes \"dog\". Both the document and the query go through the same analysis, so they match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0082","card":"q-azure-ai-023-search-types","sec":"azure-ai-s024","q":"How can vector search match \"dog\" with the German word \"hund\", or text with an image?","a":"<p>Embedding models map content with the same meaning to nearby points in a vector space. A multilingual model puts \"dog\" and \"hund\" close together, and a multimodal model can embed images and text in one space. Search then finds nearest vectors regardless of language or media.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0083","card":"q-azure-ai-024-ai-enrichment","sec":"azure-ai-s025","q":"What is AI enrichment in Azure AI Search and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>Raw content such as scanned PDFs, images and audio is not searchable text. Enrichment runs a skillset during indexing: it cracks the documents open, applies skills (OCR, entity recognition, translation, image analysis), and writes the enriched output into the index. Then you can search content that was not text before.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0084","card":"q-azure-ai-024-ai-enrichment","sec":"azure-ai-s025","q":"In what order does the Azure AI Search enrichment pipeline run?","a":"<p>Data source, then document cracking (extract text and images), then skills that transform or add data, then the enriched document is mapped to index fields, then the content can be searched. Skills need the cracked content first, and the index fields are filled last.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0085","card":"q-azure-ai-024-ai-enrichment","sec":"azure-ai-s025","q":"When would you use a custom skill in an Azure AI Search skillset?","a":"<p>When no built-in skill does the job, for example calling your own model or an Azure OpenAI embedding endpoint, or domain logic. A custom skill is a web API that receives records from the indexer and returns enriched values. Cost is that you host and secure that API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0086","card":"q-azure-ai-024-ai-enrichment","sec":"azure-ai-s025","q":"What does a translation skill in a skillset let a search do?","a":"<p>It stores a translated copy of the content in the index, so a query for \"hund\" can match a document written in English about dogs. It is a way to give multilingual search without asking users to know the document language.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0087","card":"q-azure-ai-024-ai-enrichment","sec":"azure-ai-s025","q":"How are built-in cognitive skills billed in Azure AI Search, and what limits their use on the free tier?","a":"<p>Built-in skills call Azure AI services, which are billed through an attached billable Azure AI services resource. The free search tier allows only a small daily quota of free enrichments, so larger runs need that billable resource attached. Check the current docs for the exact quota.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0088","card":"q-azure-ai-025-semantic-ranking","sec":"azure-ai-s026","q":"What does semantic ranking do in Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>It re-ranks the top results of a keyword or hybrid query using language-understanding models from Microsoft so the most meaningful results come first. It can also return captions (highlighted key passage per result) and an extracted answer. It improves relevance without changing your index data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0089","card":"q-azure-ai-025-semantic-ranking","sec":"azure-ai-s026","q":"Why does a plain keyword search for \"What is the capital of France?\" return poor results, and how does semantic ranking help?","a":"<p>Keyword search matches words and may rank pages about \"capital punishment\" in France. The semantic ranker reads the meaning of the query and passages, re-orders results to match the intent, and can extract the direct answer \"Paris\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0090","card":"q-azure-ai-025-semantic-ranking","sec":"azure-ai-s026","q":"What do you need to do to use semantic ranking in Azure AI Search, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>Enable semantic ranker on the search service (a billable feature after a free monthly query allowance) and configure a semantic configuration on the index naming the title, content and keyword fields. Then pass <code>queryType=semantic</code> in the query. The trade-off is added cost and a little latency for better relevance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0091","card":"q-azure-ai-025-semantic-ranking","sec":"azure-ai-s026","q":"Is semantic ranking the same thing as vector search in Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>No. Vector search is a retrieval method that finds candidates by embedding similarity. Semantic ranking is a re-ranking step that reorders an already retrieved set of results and adds captions and answers. They are often combined: hybrid retrieval then semantic re-ranking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0092","card":"q-azure-ai-026-vectors","sec":"azure-ai-s027","q":"What is a vector embedding, and why is it useful for search?","a":"<p>An embedding is a list of numbers produced by a model that represents the meaning of text, an image or other content. Similar meanings give vectors that are close, so you can find relevant documents by measuring distance (cosine similarity, for example), even without matching words.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0093","card":"q-azure-ai-026-vectors","sec":"azure-ai-s027","q":"What is the difference between HNSW and exhaustive KNN in Azure AI Search vector search?","a":"<p>Exhaustive KNN compares the query against every vector, so it is exact but slow on large indexes. HNSW is an approximate nearest-neighbor graph algorithm that is much faster with a small accuracy loss. Use HNSW for most production indexes and exhaustive KNN for small sets or when you need exact results, such as evaluating recall.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0094","card":"q-azure-ai-026-vectors","sec":"azure-ai-s027","q":"How do you run a vector query in Azure AI Search, and why must the query use the same embedding model as the indexed documents?","a":"<p>You first send the query text to an embeddings model to get a query vector, then send that vector to the search endpoint (or use an integrated vectorizer that does it for you). Vectors from different models live in different spaces, so distances between them are meaningless and results would be wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0095","card":"q-azure-ai-026-vectors","sec":"azure-ai-s027","q":"Why would you apply scalar or binary quantization to vectors in Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>Quantization compresses vectors to use less memory and storage, which lowers cost and speeds up search. The price is some accuracy loss, which you can reduce with oversampling and re-ranking using the original full-precision vectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0096","card":"q-azure-ai-026-vectors","sec":"azure-ai-s027","q":"What must you define when creating an index that stores vectors in Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>A vector field with its dimensions (matching the embedding model output), and a vector search configuration with an algorithm (HNSW or exhaustive KNN) and optionally compression, grouped into profiles that the field refers to. Dimensions must match the model, otherwise indexing fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0097","card":"q-azure-ai-027-demo-build-an-index-portal","sec":"azure-ai-s028","q":"In an Azure AI Search index, what do the field attributes retrievable, filterable, sortable, facetable and searchable mean?","a":"<p>Retrievable fields can be returned in results. Filterable fields can be used in exact-match filter expressions. Sortable fields can be used to order results. Facetable fields produce counts to narrow results (category, price range). Searchable fields are analyzed for full-text relevance. Each attribute adds index size, so enable only what you need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0098","card":"q-azure-ai-027-demo-build-an-index-portal","sec":"azure-ai-s028","q":"Why should you not mark every field in an Azure AI Search index as filterable, sortable and facetable?","a":"<p>Each attribute builds extra index structures, increasing storage and slowing indexing. Enable them only for fields you actually filter, sort or facet on. Changing attributes later usually means rebuilding the index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0099","card":"q-azure-ai-027-demo-build-an-index-portal","sec":"azure-ai-s028","q":"How does an Azure AI Search indexer keep an index up to date, and what does the data source need for incremental refresh?","a":"<p>The first run is a full crawl. Later runs pick up only changes, which needs change tracking in the data source (for example a high-water-mark column in SQL, or integrated change tracking, or blob last-modified). Without change tracking each run re-reads everything. Deletions need a soft-delete policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0100","card":"q-azure-ai-027-demo-build-an-index-portal","sec":"azure-ai-s028","q":"What does a suggester do in Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>It enables type-ahead suggestions and autocomplete on chosen fields as the user types. You define it on the index with the fields to use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0101","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"What does Azure AI Content Safety analyze text and images for, and how is the result expressed?","a":"<p>It scores four harm categories: hate, sexual, violence and self-harm. Each category gets a severity level, and text can score in several categories at once. Your application decides a threshold per category and blocks, flags or allows the content.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0102","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"Why does the application, not the service, decide what to block with Azure AI Content Safety?","a":"<p>The service returns severity scores. The right threshold depends on your audience and use case (a children's app is stricter than a research tool). Keeping the decision in your policy lets you tune per category without waiting for a service change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0103","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"What are Prompt Shields in Azure AI Content Safety and what attacks do they target?","a":"<p>Prompt Shields scan user prompts and attached documents sent to an LLM for jailbreak attempts (user prompt attacks) and for hidden instructions inside documents (indirect prompt injection). You check input before it reaches the model and block or flag attacks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0104","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"What does groundedness detection check?","a":"<p>Whether an LLM answer is supported by the grounding text you supplied, such as retrieved documents in a RAG app. It flags ungrounded parts, which helps catch hallucination. Asking for an explanation of the ungrounded parts adds latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0105","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"What does protected material detection do?","a":"<p>It checks LLM output for known copyrighted text (such as song lyrics or articles) and for code taken from public GitHub repositories, so you can block or cite it. It is a check on output, not on user input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0106","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"When would you use custom categories in Azure AI Content Safety?","a":"<p>When your policy has a harm type the four standard categories do not cover, such as competitor mentions or domain-specific unsafe advice. You define the category, label examples and train. A rapid option lets you react to a new emerging pattern quickly without a full retraining.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0107","card":"q-azure-ai-028-6-content-safety","sec":"azure-ai-s029","q":"Where should content safety checks sit in an LLM app, and why check both sides?","a":"<p>Check user input (before the model, using prompt shields and text analysis) and model output (before the user sees it, using text analysis, groundedness and protected material checks). Input checks stop attacks and abusive prompts. Output checks catch harmful or ungrounded replies that the model produced anyway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0108","card":"q-azure-ai-029-demo-text-moderation-exercise-05-02b","sec":"azure-ai-s030","q":"In <code>@azure-rest/ai-content-safety</code> for Node.js, what is <code>isUnexpected</code> used for after calling the analyze endpoint?","a":"<p>The REST-style client returns a response object whether the call succeeded or failed. <code>isUnexpected</code> is a type guard that is true when the status is not the expected success one, so you throw or handle the error before reading <code>result.body</code>. Skipping it means reading fields from an error body.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const result = await client.path(&quot;/text:analyze&quot;).post(analyzeTextParameters);\nif (isUnexpected(result)) {\n    throw result;\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"azure-ai-f0109","card":"q-azure-ai-029-demo-text-moderation-exercise-05-02b","sec":"azure-ai-s030","q":"In the Azure AI Content Safety text analyze response, how do you read the result for each harm category?","a":"<p>Read <code>result.body.categoriesAnalysis</code>, an array with one entry per category holding the category name and its severity. Loop over it and compare each severity with your own threshold for that category.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0110","card":"q-azure-ai-030-7-translator","sec":"azure-ai-s031","q":"What is the difference between translation and transliteration in Azure AI Translator?","a":"<p>Translation changes the language (English to Hindi). Transliteration keeps the language but changes the script, for example writing Hindi in Latin letters. Use transliteration when users type a language with a different keyboard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0111","card":"q-azure-ai-030-7-translator","sec":"azure-ai-s031","q":"When would you use Document Translation instead of the text translation API in Azure AI Translator?","a":"<p>Use Document Translation for whole files (Word, PDF, PowerPoint and others) where you want formatting preserved. It works on files in storage, and batch jobs run asynchronously. Use text translation for short strings in real time, such as chat messages or UI text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0112","card":"q-azure-ai-030-7-translator","sec":"azure-ai-s031","q":"When do you need Custom Translator, and what does it require?","a":"<p>When general translation gets your domain terms wrong, such as legal, medical or brand vocabulary. You train a model on parallel sentence pairs in both languages, evaluate it, and deploy it under a category ID. It needs good quality bilingual training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0113","card":"q-azure-ai-030-7-translator","sec":"azure-ai-s031","q":"Why would you run Azure AI Translator as a container at the edge?","a":"<p>For sites without reliable internet (ships, remote locations), strict data-residency rules, or low latency. It translates locally. The trade-off is that you manage the container, and it supports a more limited feature set than the cloud API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0114","card":"q-azure-ai-030-7-translator","sec":"azure-ai-s031","q":"How do you combine Translator with other Azure AI services in one solution?","a":"<p>For spoken translation, use Speech (the Speech service has built-in translation), or run speech to text, then Translator, then text to speech. For a chatbot, translate input to the language of your content and translate the answer back. In Foundry apps, Translator lets an LLM app accept any input language.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0115","card":"q-azure-ai-031-demo-text-translation-exercise-06-02b","sec":"azure-ai-s032","q":"In Azure AI Translator, why does the credential include a region along with the key?","a":"<p>The global Translator endpoint (and multi-service resources) needs to know which resource region the key belongs to, sent as the <code>Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Region</code> header. Without it you get an authentication error for keys on regional or multi-service resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0116","card":"q-azure-ai-031-demo-text-translation-exercise-06-02b","sec":"azure-ai-s032","q":"In <code>@azure-rest/ai-translation-text</code> for Node.js, how do you call the translate route and set the languages?","a":"<p>The body is an array of objects with a <code>text</code> field, so one call can translate many strings. <code>to</code> and <code>from</code> are query parameters with language codes. If you leave out <code>from</code>, the service detects the source language. Add several <code>to</code> values to get several translations in one call.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>translationClient.path(&quot;/translate&quot;).post({\n    body: [{ text: &quot;The world is full of miracles.&quot; }],\n    queryParameters: { to: &quot;hi&quot;, from: &quot;en&quot; },\n})</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"azure-ai-f0117","card":"q-azure-ai-032-8-document-intelligence","sec":"azure-ai-s033","q":"When do you use a pre-built model and when a custom model in Document Intelligence?","a":"<p>Use a pre-built model when your document type is covered (invoice, receipt, ID, W-2) because it needs no training and gives named fields. Use a custom model when the layout or fields are your own and need labeled sample documents. Try pre-built first because it is quicker and has no training cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0118","card":"q-azure-ai-032-8-document-intelligence","sec":"azure-ai-s033","q":"What would you check if a custom Document Intelligence model gives poor field accuracy?","a":"<p>Labeling consistency, the number and variety of training documents (different layouts and scan quality), whether the document type needs a separate model per layout, and whether the document is a fixed template or variable. Test on documents not used for training.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0119","card":"q-azure-ai-032-8-document-intelligence","sec":"azure-ai-s033","q":"How would you build an invoice processing pipeline with Azure AI services?","a":"<p>Store files in Blob Storage, trigger a function when a file arrives, call the Document Intelligence invoice model, validate the fields (use confidence scores to route uncertain ones to human review), write results to a database, and optionally index the text with Azure AI Search.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0120","card":"q-azure-ai-032-8-document-intelligence","sec":"azure-ai-s033","q":"How do Document Intelligence and Azure AI Search work together?","a":"<p>Document Intelligence turns scanned or complex documents into structured text and fields, and Search indexes that output so it can be queried. Document layout analysis is also used to chunk documents by sections and tables before embedding them for RAG.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0121","card":"q-azure-ai-033-9-azure-ai-foundry-ai-studio-and-azure-o","sec":"azure-ai-s034","q":"What is Azure AI Foundry, and which problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It is Microsoft's platform to build, evaluate, deploy and manage AI applications, with a catalog of models, prompt tooling, evaluation, safety and monitoring in one place. It handles the lifecycle that pre-built APIs do not. Naming note: it was Azure AI Studio and was renamed Azure AI Foundry in late 2024, and branding has kept evolving, so check the current name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0122","card":"q-azure-ai-033-9-azure-ai-foundry-ai-studio-and-azure-o","sec":"azure-ai-s034","q":"What are the stages of a typical machine learning project lifecycle, and where does monitoring lead?","a":"<p>Prepare data (ingest, clean, transform), split into training and validation sets, train, validate on held-out data, deploy as a secured API, and monitor in production. Monitoring finds drift or errors, which loop back to more data, retraining or tweaking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0123","card":"q-azure-ai-033-9-azure-ai-foundry-ai-studio-and-azure-o","sec":"azure-ai-s034","q":"Why do you split data into training and validation sets?","a":"<p>To measure performance on data the model has not seen. Evaluating on training data would hide overfitting, where the model memorizes instead of generalizing. A common split keeps most data for training (for example 80/20 or 70/30) and a smaller part for validation or testing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0124","card":"q-azure-ai-033-9-azure-ai-foundry-ai-studio-and-azure-o","sec":"azure-ai-s034","q":"How would you secure a model deployed as an API on Azure?","a":"<p>Put Microsoft Entra ID authentication (or managed identity) in front of it instead of sharing keys, use RBAC to limit who can call and manage it, restrict the network with private endpoints, and monitor usage. Scale it with a managed compute option such as Kubernetes so load changes do not take it down.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0125","card":"q-azure-ai-034-key-features","sec":"azure-ai-s035","q":"What is the model catalog in Azure AI Foundry, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It is a browsable list of models from OpenAI, Microsoft (Phi), Meta (Llama), Google (Gemma), Mistral and others, which you can try and deploy. It lets you compare and pick a model for the task without leaving Azure, and you are not tied to one vendor. Available models change often, so check the current catalog.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0126","card":"q-azure-ai-034-key-features","sec":"azure-ai-s035","q":"What are model benchmarks in Foundry, and how would you use them to choose?","a":"<p>They let you compare several models on quality measures and cost on standard datasets. Use them to shortlist, then evaluate on your own data, because public benchmarks may not match your task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0127","card":"q-azure-ai-034-key-features","sec":"azure-ai-s035","q":"How can Azure AI services be combined with LLMs in a Foundry application?","a":"<p>Use Content Safety to filter inputs and outputs (for example block certain topics), Translator to accept any input language, Speech for voice in and out, Vision for images, and Document Intelligence or Search to supply data. The LLM does reasoning and generation and the services handle specialized tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0128","card":"q-azure-ai-035-azure-openai","sec":"azure-ai-s036","q":"Why would an organization use Azure OpenAI Service instead of the OpenAI API directly?","a":"<p>It offers OpenAI models inside Azure, with Azure billing, Entra ID and managed identity, RBAC, private networking, regional deployment choices, content filtering, and enterprise compliance commitments. It also sits next to AKS, Cosmos DB, Blob Storage and Log Analytics. The trade-off is that new models may arrive in Azure later and you manage deployments and quotas in your subscription.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0129","card":"q-azure-ai-035-azure-openai","sec":"azure-ai-s036","q":"In Azure OpenAI, what is a deployment and why do you create one before calling a model?","a":"<p>A deployment is a named instance of a chosen model in your resource, with its own capacity (tokens per minute quota) and settings. Applications call the deployment name, not the raw model name. This lets you control capacity, versions and updates per deployment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0130","card":"q-azure-ai-035-azure-openai","sec":"azure-ai-s036","q":"How would you authenticate an application to Azure OpenAI without storing an API key?","a":"<p>Use Microsoft Entra ID with a managed identity for the app and assign it a role such as Cognitive Services OpenAI User on the resource. The SDK obtains tokens for the identity, so no key is stored or rotated by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0131","card":"q-azure-ai-035-azure-openai","sec":"azure-ai-s036","q":"What happens when an Azure OpenAI deployment exceeds its quota, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>The service returns HTTP 429 (too many requests). Retry with exponential backoff, respecting the retry header, spread traffic across deployments or regions, request more quota, or consider provisioned throughput for steady high-volume use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0132","card":"q-azure-ai-035-azure-openai","sec":"azure-ai-s036","q":"Can you use models other than OpenAI models through Azure and its tools?","a":"<p>Yes. Foundry offers models from Meta, Mistral, Microsoft and others, so the platform is not limited to OpenAI. Which are available and how they are billed varies by model and region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0133","card":"q-azure-ai-036-choosing-a-model","sec":"azure-ai-s037","q":"How would you choose a model for an application in Azure AI Foundry?","a":"<p>Start from the task and constraints (quality, latency, cost, context length, modality, data residency). Shortlist from the catalog, compare with benchmarks, then run evaluations on your own test data. Prefer the smallest model that meets the quality bar because larger models cost more and are slower. Consider fine-tuning only if prompting and RAG are not enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0134","card":"q-azure-ai-036-choosing-a-model","sec":"azure-ai-s037","q":"What are the evaluation metrics groundedness, relevance, coherence, fluency and similarity for LLM output?","a":"<p>Groundedness: is the answer supported by the provided context. Relevance: does it address the question. Coherence: does it flow logically. Fluency: is the language correct and natural. Similarity: how close is it to a reference (ground-truth) answer. Use them together because a fluent answer can still be ungrounded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0135","card":"q-azure-ai-036-choosing-a-model","sec":"azure-ai-s037","q":"What is the difference between accuracy and groundedness when you evaluate a model?","a":"<p>Accuracy compares predictions with known correct values, which suits classification or prediction tasks. Groundedness checks that an LLM answer stays within the supplied context, which suits RAG apps. A fluent, confident answer can be accurate-sounding yet ungrounded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0136","card":"q-azure-ai-036-choosing-a-model","sec":"azure-ai-s037","q":"What is a fine-tuned model, and when should you fine-tune instead of using prompts or RAG?","a":"<p>A fine-tuned model is a base model trained further on your examples. Use it for consistent style, format or a narrow task where prompting does not hold. Use RAG when the issue is access to changing facts, because fine-tuning bakes in knowledge at training time and is costly to refresh.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0137","card":"q-azure-ai-037-popular-models","sec":"azure-ai-s038","q":"What does each OpenAI model family do: GPT, embeddings, DALL-E and Whisper?","a":"<p>GPT models understand and generate text and code (newer ones accept images and audio too). Embedding models turn text into vectors for similarity search and RAG. DALL-E creates images from prompts. Whisper transcribes and translates speech in many languages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0138","card":"q-azure-ai-037-popular-models","sec":"azure-ai-s038","q":"Which model family would you pick for a RAG application, and what does each part do?","a":"<p>An embedding model to vectorize documents and queries for retrieval (stored in a vector index such as Azure AI Search), and a chat model (GPT family) to write the answer from the retrieved text. Use the same embedding model for indexing and querying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0139","card":"q-azure-ai-037-popular-models","sec":"azure-ai-s038","q":"How do BERT and GPT differ?","a":"<p>BERT is an encoder model that reads text in both directions and is used for understanding tasks such as classification and embeddings. GPT is a decoder model that predicts the next token and is used for text generation. They suit different tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0140","card":"q-azure-ai-037-popular-models","sec":"azure-ai-s038","q":"What are small language models like Microsoft Phi good for compared with large models?","a":"<p>They are smaller and cheaper to run, faster, and can run on edge devices or with tight budgets, with a quality trade-off on broad or complex tasks. Use them for focused tasks, local inference or cost-sensitive workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0141","card":"q-azure-ai-037-popular-models","sec":"azure-ai-s038","q":"What is Whisper, and what are its trade-offs versus Azure AI Speech to text?","a":"<p>Whisper is an OpenAI speech recognition model that handles many languages and can run offline or be hosted in Azure. Azure AI Speech offers real-time streaming, batch, custom models and SDKs built around speech workloads. Choose Speech for real-time and customization, Whisper for strong multilingual file transcription.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0142","card":"q-azure-ai-038-demo-chat-with-your-pdf-azure-openai-ai","sec":"azure-ai-s039","q":"How does the \"chat with your data\" pattern in Azure OpenAI use Azure AI Search?","a":"<p>Your documents are chunked and indexed in Azure AI Search. At question time the app retrieves the most relevant chunks, adds them to the prompt, and the model answers using them and can cite the references. This is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0143","card":"q-azure-ai-038-demo-chat-with-your-pdf-azure-openai-ai","sec":"azure-ai-s039","q":"Why do you chunk documents before indexing them for a RAG chat application?","a":"<p>Models have a limited context window and retrieval works best on focused passages. Chunks that are too large dilute relevance and waste tokens, while chunks that are too small lose context. Overlap between chunks helps keep meaning across boundaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0144","card":"q-azure-ai-038-demo-chat-with-your-pdf-azure-openai-ai","sec":"azure-ai-s039","q":"Why does a RAG chat give references with answers, and why is that valuable?","a":"<p>References link each statement to a passage in the source document. Users can verify, and it reduces blind trust in hallucinations. It also helps debugging when the answer is wrong, because you can see what was retrieved.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0145","card":"q-azure-ai-038-demo-chat-with-your-pdf-azure-openai-ai","sec":"azure-ai-s039","q":"Why does a blob storage account used for browser-based upload to a chat-with-your-data setup need CORS enabled?","a":"<p>The web studio runs in the browser on another origin and uploads files directly to Blob Storage. Browsers block cross-origin requests unless the storage account allows that origin through CORS rules. Without them, uploads fail with a cross-origin error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0146","card":"q-azure-ai-038-demo-chat-with-your-pdf-azure-openai-ai","sec":"azure-ai-s039","q":"How would you reduce hallucinations in a RAG chat application?","a":"<p>Instruct the model to answer only from the retrieved context and say when it does not know, improve retrieval (hybrid search, semantic ranking, good chunking), cite references, check groundedness with Content Safety, and test with questions that have no answer in the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0147","card":"q-azure-ai-039-course-summary","sec":"azure-ai-s040","q":"Which Azure AI service do you pick for: reading text from an image, transcribing a call, scoring sentiment, searching company documents, filtering harmful text, translating, and extracting invoice fields?","a":"<p>OCR (Vision) for image text. Speech to text for the call. Language for sentiment. Azure AI Search for document search. Content Safety to filter harmful text. Translator for translation. Document Intelligence for invoice fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"azure-ai-f0148","card":"q-azure-ai-039-course-summary","sec":"azure-ai-s040","q":"How would you design a multilingual voice assistant on Azure?","a":"<p>Speech to text (with language detection) captures the user's request, Translator or Speech translation converts it to the working language, an LLM in Azure OpenAI answers with retrieval from Azure AI Search, Content Safety checks input and output, and text to speech speaks the reply in the user's language. Authenticate with managed identity and keep keys out of the code.</p>","hand":true}];
